---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
const version = "v0.3.0";
const release = `https://github.com/aslilac/squill/releases/download/${version}`;
---

<Docs
	page="install"
	title="Install"
	lede="Four ways to get the binary, in rough order of how little you have to think about it."
>
	<section>
		<h2>mise</h2>
		<p>
			<a href="https://mise.jdx.dev">mise</a> and its GitHub backend pull a
			precompiled binary from the latest release, picking the one that matches
			whatever it is running on. Nothing has to know the machine&rsquo;s os or
			architecture, which is what makes it the easy one to drop into CI.
		</p>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`mise use -g github:aslilac/squill

squill fmt --check .`} />
		<p>
			Pin a version with <code>github:aslilac/squill@0.3.0</code>. mise verifies
			GitHub artifact attestations and SLSA provenance on install.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="nix">
		<h2>Nix</h2>
		<p>
			The flake exposes the binary as its default package, so there is no
			install step at all if you only want to run it once.
		</p>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`nix run github:aslilac/squill -- fmt --check .

nix shell github:aslilac/squill`} />
		<p>
			It builds from source, so it covers platforms the release matrix does
			not.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="cargo">
		<h2>Cargo</h2>
		<p>
			Cargo can clone the source, check out the release tag, and install the
			binary in one command. This is the route that needs a Rust toolchain.
		</p>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`cargo install --git https://github.com/aslilac/squill.git --tag v0.3.0 cli --bin squill`} />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Release archives</h2>
		<p>
			Every tag publishes archives to <a
				href="https://github.com/aslilac/squill/releases">GitHub releases</a
			>: Linux and Windows on x86_64 and arm64, and macOS on arm64. The tab
			for the machine you&rsquo;re reading this on opens first.
		</p>
		<CodeTabs
			tabs={[
				...[
					["x86_64", "x86_64"],
					["arm64", "aarch64"],
				].map(([arch, target]) => ({
					id: `linux-${arch}`,
					label: `linux ${arch}`,
					os: "linux",
					arch,
					lang: "sh",
					code: `curl -fsSL ${release}/squill-${version}-${target}-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz \\
	| sudo tar xz -C /usr/local/bin/
squill --version`,
				})),
				{
					id: "macos-arm64",
					label: "macos arm64",
					os: "macos",
					arch: "arm64",
					lang: "sh",
					code: `sudo mkdir -p /usr/local/bin
curl -fsSL ${release}/squill-${version}-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz \\
	| sudo tar xz -C /usr/local/bin/
squill --version`,
				},
				...[
					["x86_64", "x86_64"],
					["arm64", "aarch64"],
				].map(([arch, target]) => ({
					id: `windows-${arch}`,
					label: `windows ${arch}`,
					os: "windows",
					arch,
					lang: "powershell",
					code: `$url = "${release}/squill-${version}-${target}-pc-windows-msvc.zip"
$dir = "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\squill"
Invoke-WebRequest $url -OutFile "$env:TEMP\\squill.zip"
Expand-Archive "$env:TEMP\\squill.zip" -DestinationPath $dir -Force
# Put it on your PATH (new terminals pick it up):
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("Path",
	[Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("Path", "User") + ";$dir", "User")`,
				})),
			]}
			selected="linux-x86_64"
		/>
		<p>
			There&rsquo;s no build for Intel Macs; <a href="docs/install/#nix"
				>Nix</a
			> or <a href="docs/install/#cargo">Cargo</a> builds one from source.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Checking, not writing</h2>
		<p>
			However you install it, CI usually wants <code>--check</code>: squill
			writes nothing, prints a diff for anything that would change, and exits 1
			if there is anything to report.
		</p>
		<Code lang="sh" code={`squill fmt --check .`} />
		<p>
			Add <code>--strict</code> to also fail when a statement could not be
			parsed and was passed through verbatim &mdash; useful once you expect
			squill to understand everything in the tree.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
